Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 09:04:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make / a public landing page; move the app to /app
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Google's OAuth reviewers hit `/` with no session and got redirected
straight to /login before seeing any content — the root page was
"use client", checked auth on mount, and called
router.replace("/login") the instant session was null. A reviewer (or
a search crawler) never saw anything but a login wall.

- app/page.tsx: now a genuinely public landing page. No auth check
  gates it — same content for a logged-out visitor, a crawler, or a
  reviewer with zero session. Has the plain-text purpose description,
  a header/hero, a Sign In button, and a footer with visible links to
  /privacy and /terms. The only thing session state changes is where
  the CTA buttons point (Sign in → /login vs Go to your shop → /app),
  and that's a client-side label swap after mount, never a redirect —
  the page never navigates anyone away from `/` on its own.

- app/app/page.tsx (new): the actual authenticated shop UI, moved here
  verbatim from the old app/page.tsx. Still correctly redirects to
  /login when there's no session — that's expected and fine for a
  private route; Google's requirement was only that the *root* be
  public, not the whole app.

- components/AuthScreen.tsx: the three places that redirected to "/"
  after a session existed (on mount, after login, after signup) now go
  to "/app" instead, since that's where the app actually lives now.
  Also linked the "Terms" / "Privacy Policy" mentions in the
  by-continuing disclaimer to /terms and /privacy — they were plain
  text before, not actual links.

Verified: npm run build compiles cleanly. New route sizes: / is 1.81
kB (was carrying the full 27.8 kB app bundle before), /app now carries
that weight instead.
